---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import type { PersonData } from "../utils/extract-data.mjs";

const peopleData = (await import("../data/people.public.json")).default as PersonData[];
const people = peopleData.filter(p => p.branch === "telfer" || !p.branch) || [];

// ── Numbers on this page are COUNTED, never typed ────────────────────────────
// Mark's rule (5 Nov 2026): every number a page prints must exactly match its
// label. A methodology page that quotes stale counts is the worst possible
// page to get wrong — so every figure below is derived at build time.
const total = people.length;
// Sources live in the page body as a "## Sources" heading, not a data field.
// Verified by inspection 6 Nov 2026: people.public.json has no `sources` key at
// all — the first version of this page counted a field that doesn't exist and
// proudly printed "0 with cited sources". Counting the heading is the honest
// measure of "this page cites something".
const hasSourcesBlock = (p: PersonData) =>
  /^##\s+Sources\b/im.test(((p as any).body_markdown as string) || "");
const withSources = people.filter(hasSourcesBlock).length;
const withEvidence = people.filter(p => (p as any).evidence_level).length;
const verified = people.filter(p => (p as any).evidence_level === "verified").length;
const singleSource = people.filter(p => (p as any).single_source).length;
const pct = (n: number) => Math.round((n / total) * 100);
---

<BaseLayout
  title="How This Archive Works"
  description="How the Telfer family archive decides what is true, what a certificate proves that a family story cannot, and what happens when two sources disagree."
>
  <main class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-8">
    <article class="prose prose-lg dark:prose-invert max-w-none">
      <h1 class="font-serif text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-bold text-[var(--color-heading)] mb-2">
        How This Archive Works
      </h1>
      <p class="text-sm text-[var(--color-muted)] mb-8">
        What we count as proof, what we don't, and how to tell the difference on any page
      </p>

      <p class="lead">
        This is a family history, not a family legend. Every claim on this site is
        here because something outside this website says so — a register, a
        certificate, a headstone, a printed book. Where we don't have that, the page
        says so rather than filling the gap with a guess.
      </p>

      <p>
        That sounds obvious until you try it. Most family trees online are a mixture
        of records and other people's trees, copied back and forth until nobody can
        tell which is which. This page explains exactly where the line is drawn here,
        so you can check our work instead of taking our word for it.
      </p>

      <hr />

      <h2>Two witnesses, not one</h2>

      <p>
        A fact goes into this archive on one condition: <strong>two independent
        things agree about it.</strong> Two books. Or a book and a register. Or a
        certificate and a newspaper notice. What doesn't count is the same claim
        twice from the same place.
      </p>

      <p>
        This is stricter than it looks, because a great deal of what's online isn't
        independent at all. If four family trees agree that someone was born in 1842,
        and all four copied from the same unsourced tree, you have one witness, not
        four. Genealogy sites are full of this. The number of pages repeating a claim
        tells you nothing about whether it's true.
      </p>

      <div class="not-prose my-6 rounded-lg border-l-4 border-[var(--color-highlight)] bg-[var(--color-surface)] p-4">
        <p class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-heading)] mb-2">The rule we hold ourselves to</p>
        <p class="text-sm text-[var(--color-ink)]">
          A <em>finder</em> is never a <em>witness</em>. Someone pointing us at a record
          is useful — it is not evidence. Only the record itself counts. And this
          website is never a source for itself: a page here repeating another page here
          is one claim, not two.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>What a certificate gives you that a story can't</h2>

      <p>
        Not all sources are equal, and pretending otherwise is how people end up with
        wrong ancestors. Roughly, strongest first:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Civil registration certificates and official registers.</strong>
          A government body recorded the birth, marriage or death at the time, from
          someone who was there. These stand on their own — we don't demand a second
          copy of something the state issued.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Parish registers and census returns.</strong> Contemporaneous, but
          written by hand by a clerk who may have misheard a name. Strong, not infallible.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Newspaper notices and probate records.</strong> Dated and printed,
          which makes them checkable — but they report what someone told a journalist.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Headstones.</strong> Genuinely useful, and genuinely wrong sometimes.
          Families erected stones years later from memory. The stone often gives the
          right death year and the wrong birth year.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Printed family histories.</strong> Enormously valuable, and the
          reason this archive exists — three printed Telfer books are its backbone.
          But these were compiled by researchers who had sources we can't see, and
          transcribed by scanners that make mistakes. Treated as strong leads, cross-checked.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Family stories, letters, and other people's online trees.</strong>
          Treated as <em>leads</em>, never as facts. Worth chasing. Not worth printing
          as though it happened.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Why some people have no dates</h2>

      <p>
        You will find profiles here with a birth year and nothing else, or a name and
        no years at all. This is not laziness and it is not an oversight — it is the
        rule working. Where no record has been found, no date is printed. An empty
        field is honest; an invented one is a lie that gets copied for a hundred years.
      </p>

      <p>
        Some of these are living relatives. We withhold details about living people
        deliberately, for privacy. That is a decision, not a gap in the research, and
        we don't count it as missing evidence.
      </p>

      <h2>When two sources disagree</h2>

      <p>
        This happens constantly, and it is the most interesting part of the work.
        When it does:
      </p>

      <ol>
        <li><strong>Both versions are recorded.</strong> Neither is quietly deleted.</li>
        <li>
          <strong>The printed page beats the scan.</strong> Where a scanned book and
          the physical page disagree, the physical page wins — what we're reading is a
          machine's best guess at a 19th-century font.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>A certificate beats a compiled book.</strong> The book's author was
          working further from the event than the registrar was.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Unresolvable conflicts get flagged, not decided.</strong> If two
          equally good sources genuinely contradict each other, a person resolves it.
          A machine doesn't.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <h2>What each page tells you about itself</h2>

      <p>
        Every profile carries a rating saying how well that person is known. It's the
        honest answer to the only question a stranger actually has: <em>how much
        should I trust this page?</em>
      </p>

      <div class="not-prose my-6 overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
          <thead>
            <tr class="border-b-2 border-[var(--color-border)]">
              <th class="text-left py-2 pr-4 font-semibold text-[var(--color-heading)]">Rating</th>
              <th class="text-left py-2 font-semibold text-[var(--color-heading)]">What it means</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="[&_td]:py-2 [&_td]:align-top [&_tr]:border-b [&_tr]:border-[var(--color-border)]">
            <tr>
              <td class="pr-4 font-medium whitespace-nowrap">Verified</td>
              <td>Two independent sources agree, or a civil certificate stands alone. State it plainly.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="pr-4 font-medium whitespace-nowrap">Single source</td>
              <td>One witness only. Treated as a <em>lead</em>, and the page says so out loud.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="pr-4 font-medium whitespace-nowrap">Provisional</td>
              <td>
                Right on the evidence in hand, not yet hardened against an independent
                record. Carries its receipt — what was checked, against what.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="pr-4 font-medium whitespace-nowrap">Not recorded</td>
              <td>
                Written before ratings were compulsory. <strong>This is not a clean
                bill of health</strong> — it means nobody has rated it yet.
              </td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2>Where this archive actually stands</h2>

      <p>
        Because a methodology page is worthless if it only describes the ideal, here
        is the real state of the work. These figures are counted from the archive
        itself each time this page is built — they cannot be flattered by hand.
      </p>

      <div class="not-prose my-6 grid grid-cols-2 sm:grid-cols-4 gap-3">
        <div class="rounded-lg border border-[var(--color-border)] bg-[var(--color-card-bg)] p-4 text-center">
          <div class="text-2xl font-bold font-serif text-[var(--color-heading)]">{total}</div>
          <div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-muted)] mt-1">people in the archive</div>
        </div>
        <div class="rounded-lg border border-[var(--color-border)] bg-[var(--color-card-bg)] p-4 text-center">
          <div class="text-2xl font-bold font-serif text-[var(--color-heading)]">{withSources}</div>
          <div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-muted)] mt-1">with a cited sources section</div>
        </div>
        <div class="rounded-lg border border-[var(--color-border)] bg-[var(--color-card-bg)] p-4 text-center">
          <div class="text-2xl font-bold font-serif text-[var(--color-heading)]">{verified}</div>
          <div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-muted)] mt-1">rated verified</div>
        </div>
        <div class="rounded-lg border border-[var(--color-border)] bg-[var(--color-card-bg)] p-4 text-center">
          <div class="text-2xl font-bold font-serif text-[var(--color-heading)]">{singleSource}</div>
          <div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-muted)] mt-1">resting on one witness</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="text-sm text-[var(--color-muted)]">
        That's {withSources} of {total} people ({pct(withSources)}%) with cited sources,
        and {withEvidence} carrying a rating of any kind. The rest is real work still
        to do, and we'd rather show you the number than hide it.
      </p>

      <h2>How this is enforced, not just intended</h2>

      <p>
        A rule nobody enforces is a boast. Three things make the rules on this page
        actually bind:
      </p>

      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>Only one process may write family facts.</strong> A single scheduled
          verification pass, running once a day, is the only thing permitted to write
          a fact into the archive. Everything else — research, OCR, cross-checks —
          feeds it and cannot write.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>No rating, no page.</strong> A profile whose content changes without
          an evidence rating fails the build. It cannot be published. That gate runs
          on every rebuild, so it isn't a promise someone has to remember.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Every judgement is dated and receipted.</strong> When something is
          recorded as provisional, the archive stores what was checked against what,
          and when. A decision that can't be audited later was never properly made.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <h2>When we get it wrong</h2>

      <p>
        We do, and there's a running corrections log recording every mistake found and
        what was changed. Errors here have come from scanners misreading names,
        transcribers mishearing a clerk, and — more than once — from me trusting a
        single source I should have cross-checked.
      </p>

      <p>
        If you spot something wrong, please tell us. A correction with a source
        attached is the most useful thing anyone can send. If you <em>are</em> the
        source — you have the certificate, you were there, you knew them — say so, and
        we'll treat that as the starting point for finding the record that backs it up.
      </p>

      <hr />

      <p class="text-sm text-[var(--color-muted)]">
        This archive is compiled and maintained by one person, checked against the
        sources named on each page. It is not a commercial product and makes no
        claim to be a scholarly publication. Every rating on it is a statement about
        evidence, and every one is open to challenge.
      </p>
    </article>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
